  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/c/cc/Avengers_Vol_1_1.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Loki, banished to the Isle of Silence, mentally reaches out to Earth to find a menace to confront Thor (and thus draw him back to Asgard), sees the Hulk, and tricks him into endangering a train so that he'll take the blame, and then be hunted down by Thor.&nbsp; Instead, the lumbering Hulk actually <i>saves</i> the train, but it still looks as if he was the one that threatened it, exactly as Loki intended.\n\nNews of the would-be wrecker reaches Rick Jones, who refuses to believe that the Hulk would attack a train.&nbsp; After reading the news stories in the paper, he contacts his Teen Brigade (formed in [LINK:1963:HULK_6|HULK #6]) to try and summon heroes with enough power to stop the Hulk -- the Fantastic Four.&nbsp; But Loki doesn't want the FF interfering, so he re-routes the radio waves toward Dr. Don Blake's office.\n\nWhat Loki does <i>not</i> anticipate is that the rerouted waves reach others as well, including Hank Pym and Tony Stark.&nbsp; Soon, Ant-Man, Wasp, and Iron Man are all heading to the \"Southwest\" (which is a <i>thousand</i> miles away from NYC, as stated on page 5).&nbsp; Hours later, the FF finally detect and respond to the Teen Brigade's call for help, but as they're currently involved in another case, they suggest that <i>others</i> might be able to help.&nbsp; At that moment, the teenagers are joined all at once by Thor, Ant-Man, Wasp and Iron Man.\n\nLoki is upset that the others are complicating things and manages to lure Thor out on his own, chasing down a mental image of the Hulk he cast.&nbsp; Thor realizes it must be Loki and heads off to Asgard to have a look.&nbsp; But that leaves the others to track down the Hulk, and sure enough, he is spotted eventually by an ant, who reports back to Ant-Man.&nbsp; They track Hulk to the circus that he's been hiding in as a performer, and there is a brief scuffle, but Hulk manages to get free and head off.\n\nMeanwhile, in Asgard, Thor requests that Odin allow him to visit Loki on his island.&nbsp; Loki has prepared many traps of course, but eventually Thor and Loki face each other.&nbsp; But Loki's sets loose the \"Silent Ones\" -- enemies of the gods!&nbsp; The Trolls!&nbsp; Traps, trolls and tricks don't stop Thor, who then curiously decides to take him back to Earth rather than face Odin.&nbsp; \"There, you will find others who are waiting for you... others almost as powerful as <i>I</i>!\"\n\nBack on Earth, Iron Man is still chasing after the Hulk, who is heading toward Detroit, and they face off inside of an auto-factory.&nbsp; But Thor drops in with Loki and explains how he tried to frame the Hulk to lure Thor into battle with the behemoth.&nbsp; Loki makes himself radioactive and orders Hulk and Iron Man to flee before they're killed by the radiation, allowing he and Thor to resume hostilities, but Ant-Man and Wasp arrive on the scene, and activate a trapdoor that sends Loki down into a lead-lined tank, which will protect everyone until Loki can no longer continue, which will give Thor a chance to return him to where he belongs.\n\nBut before he departs, Ant-Man proposes that they form a team.&nbsp; \"If we combine forces, we could be almost unbeatable!\"&nbsp; Iron Man is all for it, and Thor agrees that they could do much good.&nbsp; Hulk is tired of being pursued, so he also agrees, and Jan proposes the name \"Avengers,\" which sticks.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "<b>Chronology Note:</b>\n[BR] Loki's presence on the so-called \"Isle of Silence\" seems to be out of step with where we saw him <i>last</i> time, in May's [LINK:1963:JIM_94_thor0|JIM #94], where we found him chained to a wall on a cliff with \"uru menacles [sic:&nbsp; should be <i>manacles</i>]\" near Asgard (see page 4 of [LINK:1963:JIM_94_thor0|JIM #94]).&nbsp; He's even shown to still be there on page 8 of <i>this</i> month's [LINK:1963:JIM_96_thor0|JIM #96]!\n\nSo the natural question is:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Exactly <i>when</i> did this first Avengers story take place?]\n\nI <b><i>get</i></b> it -- it's a story, not to mention a very groundbreaking one, and I've stated many times that doing the chronology two-step is <i>not</i> something that Longbox Playback chooses to engage in.&nbsp; I instead plow forward in strict publication-order sequence, as explained [PAGE: reading-order | here].&nbsp; But while I choose not to penalize the story for the inconsistency (because of the obvious <i>intent</i>), I still am allowed to [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=scratch my head]...\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I could even go so far as to suggest that this story takes place <i>after</i> the events of JIM issue 96, but as there's no direct reference, I'm forced to stick with the reading order rule -- which states that the JIM issue was, in fact, published one day <i>after</i> this issue.)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | LokisRovingEyes | width=12%] The 22-page story itself reads far more like a Thor solo tale, with everyone else simply getting swept up in the action.&nbsp; Thor and Loki, <i>without</i> the others, consume a <i>huge</i> chunk of the pages.&nbsp; Loki provides the catalyst to get everyone together, with the Hulk being the perfect foil.\n[BR]\n[BR] (Incidentally, Jack Kirby does a <i>fantastic</i> job showing Loki's eyes roving over Earth -- <i>clearly</i> it's an <i>effect</i>, rather than literal eyes, but showing them in the background of virtually every panel is nonetheless very effective.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=Great job, Jack!])\n\nWhile Loki is able to re-route the radio waves <i>away</i> from the Baxter Building in order to prevent the meddlesome Fantastic Four from getting involved, what he <i>doesn't</i> realize is who <i>else</i> heard the broadcast.&nbsp; Within moments, it's <i>far more than Thor</i> who sets out to stop what appears to be an out-of-control Hulk, and the Teen Brigade is suddenly surrounded by an assortment of heroes, all of whom have come out west to see if they can help:\n[ASSET: center | OtherHeroesArrive | width=67%]\n\nWhile Loki <i>does</i> succeed in finally luring Thor back to Asgard, what he doesn't count on is the fact that the <i>other</i> heroes are still in the Southwest and still have their sights on tracking down the Hulk, and this is the point at which the book suffers the most, as it shows the Hulk posing as the robot \"Mechano\" in a circus, hiding his true identity so as to give him time to figure out his next step.&nbsp; And I have to say that, even while the <i>Hulk</i> magazine failed to ever really establish what the Hulk did during his down time, I can imagine that <i>no reader</i> would have predicted this!\n\n[ASSET: left | EverSpyingAnts | width=15%] Not only that, but if the Hulk was \"hiding\" under cover of the circus, what was he doing on pages 2 and 3, where he's shown jump leaping around the countryside?&nbsp; (And while I'm on that topic, since when has the Hulk ever worried about whether a train was about to crash?)\n[BR]\n[BR] All this highlights once again how poorly the Hulk has been portrayed as a character.&nbsp; His actions are random and impossible to predict, largely because Stan and Jack never bothered to establish him.&nbsp; Wouldn't it be far more likely for Dr. Banner to have reconnected with the US army and try to be near Betty Ross?\n\nThe fact of the matter is that Stan and Jack don't seem worried about that -- the Hulk is the issue's foil from start to finish, and thanks to the trillions of ever-spying ants, Ant-Man is alerted, leading to the inevitable confrontation that consumes about 6 pages of story (pages 9-14), all while Thor is up in Asgard with the intent of confronting his evil brother.\n[ASSET: center | SixPages | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: right | LokiBackToEarth | width=12%] Why Thor chooses to return Loki to Earth after spending four-and-a-half more pages of story evading traps and ultimately capturing him (as opposed to simply presenting him to Odin) -- this all makes very little sense.&nbsp; What appears to have been more important is the fact that, during all the mayhem, Jack has managed to consume about [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=half the entire book] on little more than theater, and the actual plot that kind of ended on page 7 has leapfrogged to page 19.\n\nThe next thing you know, the action resumes for <i>another</i> two pages with Iron Man in pursuit of the Hulk, who is shown leaping his way to Detroit.&nbsp; Inside the auto factory, the human members of the team battle on before Thor shows up with Loki in tow.&nbsp; Lo and behold, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=there's only two pages left!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But... I'm betting that was the <i>goal</i>...)]\n[ASSET: center | HulkVsIronMan | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: left | LokiNotDone | width=12%] [ASSET: right | LeadLinedTank | width=12%] Of course, even <i>then</i>, Loki still believes he can drive away the others by making himself radioactive, leaving just himself and Thor to settle the matter between the two of them.\n[BR]\n[BR] Loki conveniently stands in the one place where Ant-Man can instruct his ants to trap him inside a lead-lined tank, giving the five heroes a final moment to speak about the possible formation of a new super-team.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt's not a <i>great</i> origin tale, and as I said above, there are large chunks of it that come across as a solo Thor adventure, where the other heroes are simply along for the ride, but... that being <i>said</i>, a team must begin <i>somewhere</i>, and even though the story itself probably wasn't even up to mid-grade standards (due to vast swaths of it being wasted pages of needless battle action), it nevertheless gets extra points for the thrill of seeing all the characters we've been reading about these last few months (save Spider-Man and the Fantastic Four of course) actually come together.\n\nOne can argue that the joining of these characters may seem a bit <i>artificial</i> on the surface, but it at <i>least</i> promises to be an interesting mix, particularly having the Hulk around, who is highly unpredictable (partially due to the lack of depth given to him as a character in his own right).\n\n[ASSET: left| WaspReactions | width=12%] I especially liked Jan's commentary on all the other members (e.g. Thor being \"dreamy\" and Iron Man \"horrible\"), as she continues to try and make Hank jealous -- it's <i>completely</i> in character for her.\n\nI also liked the way that Loki, to the <i>very end</i>, treated the other heroes with [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=absolute disdain].&nbsp; They were simply in his way, and even as late as page 21, he just wanted them to <i>be gone</i> so that he and Thor could get down to the serious business of beating each other's brains out, even calling the Hulk a \"human dolt!\".&nbsp; This, too, was <i>absolutely</i> in character (and I loved it).&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Not to mention the fact that it very much mimics the hilarious interaction between he and the Hulk in the first <i>Avengers</i> MCU movie.&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>]\n\nHaving seen many issues now of Jack Kirby artwork, it's clear he did the <i>full penciling</i> on this one, as everyone had that <i>unmistakable</i> Kirby look and treatment, likely leaving the inker with lighter duties this time around, as if he wanted to make sure the book got off to a good start.&nbsp; (And let's be <i>honest</i>:&nbsp; from an artistic perspective, it did...)\n\nThat final panel of the five heroes together, with both Iron Man and Thor's arms raised, was especially memorable:&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: And has been paid homage many times over the decades since.]\n[ASSET: center | AvengersIsBorn | width=50%]\n\nIt's a decent start, and a just-above-mid-grade rating seems about right here.&nbsp; An origin story that is absolutely <i>necessary</i>, but outside of the obvious significance it has as the beginning of a new book-length series, was otherwise surprisingly unremarkable as an actual read.\n\nFinally... whatever happened to the Teen Brigade?&nbsp; Did someone inform them that the Hulk has been corralled?\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Or were we just supposed to just <i>overlook</i> that little detail?)]",
  "side_note": [
    "One also is forced to wonder if Hulk's \"I pity the guy who tries to beat us!\" ultimately influenced the catch phrase \"I pity the fool...(<i>fill in the blank</i>)\" of a once-famous 1980's TV character!"
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "I confess my affection and bias for the Avengers as a book, but I don't remember ever reading this story before.&nbsp; If I did, it was many decades ago, so it will be interesting starting over from the beginning like everyone else back in the Silver Age days and seeing the team morph over time.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
